Ask the cardinal directions before the diagonal ones.

lisgd matches a swipe by testing the eight directions in ascending angle order
and taking the first whose band -- the direction plus or minus the -r leniency
-- contains it. Past a leniency of 22.5 degrees those bands overlap, and because
the diagonals are interleaved with the cardinals a diagonal claims part of the
range belonging to the cardinal tested after it.

The window that matters here is right-to-left. At -r 40, URDL (225 degrees)
covers 185..265 and so takes everything below 265, leaving RL (270) with
265..310: 40 degrees of leniency on one side and 5 on the other. Since a thumb
swiping in from the screen edge arcs, most real swipes landed in the stolen
range and came out as a diagonal that nothing was bound to -- in one verbose
capture, ten of twelve edge swipes. Raising -r made this worse rather than
better, which is the opposite of what the flag reads like it does.

Testing the four cardinals first gives each its full symmetric band and leaves
the diagonals the gaps in between. At -r 45 the cardinals tile the whole circle,
so every swipe resolves to one of them and none is silently discarded.

Applies on top of lisgd-export-gesture-coords.patch.

--- a/lisgd.c
+++ b/lisgd.c
@@ -116,21 +127,30 @@ gesturecalculateswipe(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1, int mindistanc
 	dist = sqrt(pow(x1 - x0, 2) + pow(y1 - y0, 2));
 
 	if (verbose)
 		fprintf(stderr, "Swipe distance=[%.2f]; degrees=[%.2f]\n", dist, degrees);
 
 	if (dist < mindistance) return -1;
+	/* Cardinals before diagonals. Each direction matches a symmetric band of
+	 * +/- degreesleniency, so past 22.5 the bands overlap and, tested in
+	 * ascending order, a diagonal claims angles that belong to the cardinal
+	 * after it. At -r 40 that left a right-to-left swipe accepting 265..310
+	 * degrees -- 40 degrees of slack on one side and 5 on the other -- because
+	 * URDL (225) reached up to 265 and was asked first. Real swipes arc, so
+	 * most of them landed in the stolen range and matched a diagonal nobody
+	 * had bound. Asking the cardinals first gives each one its full symmetric
+	 * band and leaves the diagonals whatever is left over. */
 	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 0))   return SwipeDU;
-	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 45))  return SwipeDLUR;
 	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 90))  return SwipeLR;
-	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 135)) return SwipeULDR;
 	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 180)) return SwipeUD;
-	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 225)) return SwipeURDL;
 	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 270)) return SwipeRL;
-	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 315)) return SwipeDRUL;
 	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 360)) return SwipeDU;
+	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 45))  return SwipeDLUR;
+	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 135)) return SwipeULDR;
+	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 225)) return SwipeURDL;
+	else if (gesturecalculateswipewithindegrees(degrees, 315)) return SwipeDRUL;
 
 	return -1;
 }
 
 Distance
 gesturecalculatedistance(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1, Swipe swipe) {
